    {
      "id": "08",
      "source_unit": "float-math",
      "title": "Rounding a Float",
      "sidebar_tag": "round()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a float and a whole number of decimal places, and print the float",
        "<strong>rounded</strong> to that many decimal places using the built-in",
        "<code>round()</code> function.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3.14159",
        "2",
        "3.14</pre>",
        "<p><code>round(value, places)</code> takes two arguments: the number to",
        "round, and how many digits to keep after the decimal point.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "# TODO: read a float value and a whole number of decimal places, then print the rounded value"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3.14159",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "3.14"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "2.71828",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "2.718"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "12.3456",
            "1"
          ],
          "expected": "12.3"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0.6667",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "0.67"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "9.8765",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "9.877"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "09",
      "source_unit": "float-math",
      "title": "Mixing Int and Float",
      "sidebar_tag": "int + float",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a whole number (as an <code>int</code>) and a decimal number (as a",
        "<code>float</code>), and print their sum.</p>",
        "<p>In Python, combining an <code>int</code> and a <code>float</code> in the",
        "same expression automatically produces a <code>float</code> &mdash; even",
        "though only one of the two values had a decimal point to begin with.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "2.5",
        "5.5</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "# TODO: read a whole number and a decimal number, then print their sum"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "2.5"
          ],
          "expected": "5.5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "0.5"
          ],
          "expected": "10.5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0",
            "4.25"
          ],
          "expected": "4.25"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "100",
            "0.75"
          ],
          "expected": "100.75"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "5",
            "5.0"
          ],
          "expected": "10.0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "source_unit": "float-math",
      "title": "The Floating-Point Precision Quirk",
      "sidebar_tag": "quirk",
      "description": [
        "<p>Use <code>print()</code> to output exactly the result of the following",
        "expression, with no input needed:</p>",
        "<pre>0.1 + 0.2</pre>",
        "<p>You might expect this to print <code>0.3</code> &mdash; run it and see",
        "what actually happens.</p>",
        "<p>Computers store floats in binary, and most decimal fractions (like 0.1)",
        "can't be represented <em>exactly</em> in binary, the same way 1/3 can't be",
        "written exactly as a decimal. The tiny error is usually too small to",
        "notice, but it shows up here because we're printing the full, unrounded",
        "value.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "# TODO: print the result of 0.1 + 0.2"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [],
          "expected": "0.30000000000000004"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
